{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310550,"nonce":"FkwhL7gRKNQdRxmIWYif3bTsMCo=","title":"FIXED 9bf7e45a 2026-09-05 (two faults on the manifest RESTORE path). Was: OPEN — three cases PASS on a fresh compile and FAIL with the ...","message":"Two faults, both on the manifest restore path in compilation.rs:\n1. `register_type_alias_from_blade` / `register_abstract_from_blade` resolved their target with NO\n   type parameters in scope and built the node with `type_args: vec![]` — while\n   `declare_class_from_blade` (:2145) and the enum path (:2429) already called\n   `register_manifest_type_params`. So `KeyValueIterator\u003cK,V\u003e = Iterator\u003c{key:K, value:V}\u003e` restored\n   with K and V unresolved AND with nothing to zip an argument against. Both now register their\n   parameters, resolve the target inside that scope, and keep the ordered ids on the node. Fixes\n   g4a/g4b. (This is what my two earlier `alias_bindings` attempts were groping at from the WRONG\n   END — the params were never registered, so no lookup could find them.)\n2. The merge-time redirect from an empty `__vtable_dispatch_thunk__*` to the real same-named thunk\n   (:7360) covered ONLY thunks. A manifest-restored method that is also compiled in the same program\n   makes the same shape: `StringTools.iterator` bound to a bodyless `StringIterator.hasNext` and\n   trapped (g2d). The redirect is now keyed on `qualified_name.unwrap_or(name)` and covers any\n   bodyless function a compiled one answers for; an extern has no non-empty counterpart so it is\n   untouched.\nBattery 449, LOST 0, both compile paths; cached probes 48 match / 9 differ (all harness KNOWN-BUGs).\n\n`--no-cache` turns off `enable_cache`, which gates TWO unrelated things: the BLADE **symbol\nmanifest** (`load_stdlib_symbols`, compilation.rs:978) and the on-disk **MIR artifact cache**\n(`try_load_blade_cached`, :1277). Only the first is responsible:\n| experiment (g4a, oracle `n=2 sum=3`) | result |\n| fresh `--no-cache` | n=2 sum=3 |\n| cached, `.rayzor` WIPED first | garbage — so no on-disk artifact was involved |\n| cached again, `.rayzor` now warm | garbage (same) |\n| cached + `RAYZOR_IGNORE_EMBEDDED_SNAPSHOT=1` | garbage — the embedded MIR snapshot is NOT it |\n| cached + a deliberately UNREADABLE `.rayzor/blade/stdlib/stdlib.bsym` | **n=2 sum=3** |\nThe last row is decisive: making `load_symbol_manifest` fail forces the on-demand stdlib path and the\ndefect disappears. So this is the **manifest RESTORE path**, the same disease as\n[[bugs-stdlib-artifacts-are-identical-restore-is-not]] (\"the miscompile is RESTORE-vs-COMPILE, not\nthe artifact\") and [[bugs-bsym-manifest-binds-bare-names-shadowing-user-types]].\nNOTE the manifest CAN carry a structure: `BladeType::Anonymous` exists (compiler/bsym/src/lib.rs:102)\nand `resolve_blade_type` rebuilds it (compilation.rs:2686). What it cannot carry is the BINDING of a\ntype parameter — `{key:K, value:V}` restores with K and V as unresolved paths — which is why the\nannotated form works and the inferred one does not.\n\n**How to see them:** `MODE=\"\" $SP/run_probe.sh $SP/STDLIB_REGRESSION/cases/g4a_stringmap_kvi_explicit_SIGSEGV`\n(MODE=\"\" = cached; MODE=\"--no-cache\" = fresh, which PASSES). The gate now runs both paths.\n\n**g4a/g4b — the anon pair read as a struct.** `while (it.hasNext()) { var kv = it.next(); s += kv.value; }`\nover `m.keyValueIterator()`. Both paths build the pair identically (`rayzor_anon_new` +\n`rayzor_anon_set_field_by_index`), and `MapKeyValueIterator.next` is byte-identical apart from a key\ncast. The consumer differs:\n  fresh : `$64 = rayzor_anon_clone($63); $67 = rayzor_anon_get_field_by_index($65, 1)`  -\u003e 3\n  cached: `$66 = gep i64 $64, [4]; $67 = load i64 $66`                                   -\u003e garbage\nSo the receiver's type does not reach `lower_field_access`'s Anonymous arm (access.rs:571+, which\nalready recovers a \"synthetic Class carrying the typedef's qualified name\"); instead the name-based\n`resolve_field_index_candidates` matched \"value\" at index 4 on some OTHER class and won.\nPROOF IT IS THE INFERRED TYPE: annotating the binding fixes it —\n`var kv:{key:String, value:Int} = it2.next();` gives 3 in BOTH paths ($SP/kvcache: infer=garbage,\nannot=3, forin=3, lit=7). So `KeyValueIterator\u003cK,V\u003e.next()`'s return does not resolve to the\nAnonymous in the cached spelling; the fix belongs where that return type is resolved, or the\nAnonymous arm has to win over the name-matched index.\n\n**g2d — StringIterator never compiled.** `for (c in StringTools.iterator(\"abc\"))` traps with\n\"`haxe.iterators.StringIterator.hasNext` was never compiled, and something called it\" (exit 134),\ni.e. a by-name forward-ref stub that never got a body. Fresh: sum=294.\n\nSame family as the two fixed in this sweep ([[project-open-defect-sweep-2026-09-05]]): the CACHED\nspelling of a structural typedef loses what the fresh one keeps. See\n[[bugs-structural-typedefs-are-class-symbols]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_cached_spelling_only_defects` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}